WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.405
Chummer6 should not ask players to memorize a shelf of labels before they know why the product matters.

2
00:00:06.405 --> 00:00:12.810
It starts with one table: runners, rules, scenes, people, and consequences in reach. Some areas are base product workbenches.

3
00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:19.214
ALICE helps with builds and tradeoffs. Origin Dossier turns the life behind the stats into contacts, enemies, debts, scars, and secrets.

4
00:00:19.214 --> 00:00:25.619
Table Pulse keeps campaign pressure bounded and playable. Runsite makes a location feel like a problem, not just a map.

5
00:00:25.619 --> 00:00:32.024
Runbook Press turns approved campaign material into primers, packets, and season books.

6
00:00:32.024 --> 00:00:38.429
Jackpoint makes aftermath easier to return to when the next session begins.

7
00:00:38.429 --> 00:00:44.834
Some ideas are expansion bets, and they should be named honestly. NEXUS-PAN is device continuity. KARMA FORGE is table-approved house-rule change.

8
00:00:44.834 --> 00:00:51.238
BLACK LEDGER is the living city: heat, factions, jobs, news, and fallout that remembers the crew.

9
00:00:51.238 --> 00:00:57.643
Other areas are clearer when they are called what they are: campaign memory, mission-space prep, publishing, community, and specialized play modes.

10
00:00:57.643 --> 00:01:04.048
That distinction matters because new users need a front door, not a vocabulary test.

11
00:01:04.048 --> 00:01:10.453
The workbench should explain what helps tonight, what grows the campaign, and what belongs to a later expansion.

12
00:01:10.453 --> 00:01:16.857
Each video, guide, and public page should make the same promise in a different register: this is a practical campaign operating system, not a pile of disconnected experiments.

13
00:01:16.857 --> 00:01:23.262
The cleaner promise is simple: build clearly, run reliably, remember consequences, and publish only what the table approves.

14
00:01:23.262 --> 00:01:29.667
The product gets easier to understand because the names serve the work instead of competing with it.
